<section class="section" id="return-distribution">
  <div class="section-label">Section 04</div>
  <h2>Return Distribution &amp; Tail Risk</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Gold's daily return distribution exhibits significant fat tails (excess kurtosis 7.02) and mild negative skew (−0.47), indicating that extreme down moves occur more frequently than a normal distribution would predict.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-wrapper" style="margin-bottom:0">
      <div class="chart-title">Daily Return Distribution vs. Normal</div>
      <div class="chart-sub">Empirical histogram vs. fitted normal curve. The excess peak at zero and fat tails are characteristic of financial returns.</div>
      <canvas id="histChart" height="220"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper" style="margin-bottom:0">
      <div class="chart-title">Tail Risk Quantiles</div>
      <div class="chart-sub">Value at Risk and expected tail loss at key confidence levels</div>
      <table class="analysis-table" style="margin-top:12px">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Percentile</th><th>Daily Return</th><th>Annualized Impact</th><th>Risk Label</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1st (VaR 99%)</td><td class="negative">−3.10%</td><td class="negative">−49.5% ann. equiv.</td><td class="negative">Extreme loss</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5th (VaR 95%)</td><td class="negative">−1.76%</td><td class="negative">−36.4% ann. equiv.</td><td class="negative">Severe loss</td></tr>
          <tr><td>25th</td><td class="negative">−0.56%</td><td class="neutral">—</td><td class="neutral">Normal down day</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50th (Median)</td><td class="positive">+0.05%</td><td class="neutral">—</td><td class="positive">Slight positive drift</td></tr>
          <tr><td>75th</td><td class="positive">+0.64%</td><td class="neutral">—</td><td class="neutral">Normal up day</td></tr>
          <tr><td>95th</td><td class="positive">+1.71%</td><td class="neutral">—</td><td class="neutral">Strong rally day</td></tr>
          <tr><td>99th</td><td class="positive">+2.82%</td><td class="neutral">—</td><td class="neutral">Extreme rally</td></tr>
          <tr style="background:rgba(231,76,60,0.07)"><td><strong>Worst Single Day</strong></td><td class="negative">≈−6%+</td><td class="negative">—</td><td class="negative">Sep 2011 crash</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div style="margin-top:16px;padding:14px;background:rgba(231,76,60,0.08);border:1px solid rgba(231,76,60,0.2);border-radius:8px;font-size:12px;color:var(--text-dim);">
        <strong style="color:#E74C3C">Tail Risk Warning:</strong> Excess kurtosis of 7.02 means the empirical 99th-percentile loss (3.10%) is substantially larger than the normal-distribution prediction (~2.59%). Standard VaR models using normality assumptions will underestimate gold's tail risk by a significant margin. Fat-tailed models (Student-t, stable distributions) are recommended.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-grid" style="margin-top:24px;">
    <div class="insight-box">
      <div class="insight-title">Excess Kurtosis = 7.02</div>
      <p>A kurtosis of 7.02 (excess) places gold firmly in "leptokurtic" territory. The 1% daily VaR is <strong>3.10%</strong> — versus 2.59% predicted by a normal model. This 20% underestimation is material for risk capital calculations.</p>
    </div>
